-- title:
--   Double-coset counts for torsion-free subgroups of SL₂(ℤ)
-- statement:
--   Let $\Gamma$ be a subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ of finite index, and assume that the action of $\pm\Gamma$ on the upper half plane has no fixed points beyond the unavoidable ones: for every $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma$ or $-\gamma \in \Gamma$, and every $\tau$ in the upper half plane, $\gamma \cdot \tau = \tau$ forces $\gamma = 1$ or $\gamma = -1$. Then two numerical identities hold simultaneously. Writing $S = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -1 \\ 1 & 0\end{pmatrix}$ and $T = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$ for the standard generators `ModularGroup.S` and `ModularGroup.T`, and $\langle g \rangle$ for the subgroup of integer powers of $g$: first, three times the number of double cosets $\Gamma g \langle ST\rangle$ in $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ equals the index of the subgroup generated by $\Gamma$ together with $-1$, i.e. of $\pm\Gamma$; second, twice the number of double cosets $\Gamma g \langle S \rangle$ equals that same index. Both double-coset counts are taken as cardinalities of the quotient of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ by the double-coset relation for the underlying sets of $\Gamma$ and of $\langle ST\rangle$, respectively $\langle S\rangle$.
--
--   This is the group-theoretic content of the assertion that a modular curve attached to a subgroup acting freely on the upper half plane modulo $\pm 1$ has no elliptic points, the two equalities expressing $\nu_3 = 0$ and $\nu_2 = 0$ in the form of exact double-coset counts against the index of $\pm\Gamma$. It is used in the computation of the genus of modular curves of full level.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Matrix_SpecialLinearGroup_three_mul_natCard_doubleCoset_eq_index_and_two_mul_of_forall_smul_eq.lean

